#text(size:16pt,weight:"bold")[The chart cost has upper and lower bounds]

Let p be prime, H² below p, and use positive primitive pairs u,v in[1,H].
Their rational reductions form S. Reduction is injective: equality mod p
makes p divide u v'−u' v, whose absolute value is at most H²−1. Hence the
integer determinant is0, and positive primitive representations agree.

Every nonprimitive pair has a common divisor d≥2. Therefore
#raw("H² − |S| ≤ sum(d=2..H) floor(H/d)²
         ≤ H² [1/4 + sum(d=3..H) 1/(d(d−1))]
         ≤ 3H²/4.  Thus H²/4 ≤ |S| ≤ H².",block:true)
H=1 is immediate. For fixed x, uniform tau makes x−tau uniform. Independent
offsets in s=m−2 slots retain EVERY fixed finite tuple with probability at
least q=(H²/(4p))^s. No selected-output or curve-distribution assumption.

An iid pool of N vectors misses at least one tuple with probability at most
p^s exp(−qN). Conversely one vector covers only |S|^s≤H^(2s) tuples, so a
pool covering all p^s tuples needs N H^(2s)≥p^s. Set L=sqrt(p)/H exactly:
#raw("Necessary: N ≥ L^(2s).
Sufficient with probability ≥1−epsilon:
 N ≥ ceil((4L²)^s [s ln(p)+ln(1/epsilon)]).",block:true)
These are HAND counting/probability arguments, with the prior generic
finite-pool logic available for reuse. No new compiled theorem is asserted.
Simultaneous coverage allows adaptive reuse after one fixed pool is drawn.
The universe is all FINITE tuples; infinity/path exceptions stay separate.

*Feasible shrink window.* For fixed epsilon and L≥2, the universal pool has
subexponential size precisely when s ln(L)=o(ln(p)). In particular:
#table(columns:(1.2fr,1.7fr),stroke:0.4pt,inset:5pt,
 [*Height*],[*Coverage cost*],
 [sqrt(p)/2],[exp(O(m)) times a logarithmic universe factor],
 [sqrt(p)/(log p)^A],[exp(O(m loglog p)); subexponential at m≈sqrt(log p/loglog p)],
 [sqrt(p)/B^kappa],[At least B^(2kappa(m−2)); if B^m≈p and m grows, p^(2kappa+o(1))])
This lower bound concerns these universal rational boxes, not every selector
or a restricted promise set. With H=floor(sqrt(p)/2), p≥16 gives q≥64^(−s).
Coverage does not prove a useful lattice equation exists.

